<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enrico De Nicola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enrico De Nicola was a Neapolitan jurist and liberal politician who became provisional head of state after Italians voted to abolish the monarchy in June 1946. When the new constitution came into force on 1 January 1948 he became the first President of the Italian Republic, serving until May 1948. He later presided over the Senate and was the first president of the Constitutional Court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enrico-de-nicola.jpg" alt="Enrico De Nicola" width="330" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presidenza della Repubblica · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enrico_De_Nicola_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Enrico De Nicola was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and became known as a penal lawyer, graduating in law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-naples-federico-ii">University of Naples</a> in 1896. A Liberal, he was first elected a deputy in 1909. He was Under-Secretary of State for the Colonies in the Giolitti government from November 1913 to March 1914, and for the Treasury in the Orlando cabinet in 1919. On 26 June 1920 he was elected speaker of the Chamber of Deputies, an office he held until January 1924. With the advent of fascism he retired from political life. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> appointed him senator in 1929, but he refused to take his seat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>De Nicola returned to his law practice and took an interest in politics again only after the fall of Italian Fascism. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> fell from power in 1943, the king tried to separate the monarchy from the Fascist regime, and De Nicola was perhaps the most influential mediator in the transition that followed. Victor Emmanuel later abdicated, Umberto became king as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-ii">Umberto II</a>, and a referendum was won by the republicans. The Constituent Assembly elected De Nicola Provisional Head of State on 28 June 1946 with 80 percent of the votes at the first round. Giulio Andreotti recalled that he was modest and hesitated over accepting.</p><p>On 25 June 1947 he resigned, citing health reasons, but the Constituent Assembly re-elected him the next day. After the Italian Constitution took effect he was formally named President of the Italian Republic on 1 January 1948. He declined to stand in the first constitutional election that May, in which Luigi Einaudi was elected. In 1948 De Nicola became a senator for life as a former head of state, and he was later elected President of the Senate and of the Constitutional Court. He died at Torre del Greco, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, on 1 October 1959. He was unmarried and had no children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enrico De Nicola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 2 June 1946 Italians voted in a referendum to abolish the monarchy of the Kingdom of Italy, and <b>Enrico De Nicola</b> became provisional head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-republic">Italian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Torre del Greco, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Enrico De Nicola"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5912 4022h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5912 4022h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
